{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the recent 10–20d slope is positive, the model consistently prices too low, and the error grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, large by days 10–30). Apply a horizon-increasing uplift in trending regimes.",
    "Lag in regime shifts: after a breakout (new 20–30d high), the model follows the move but with insufficient acceleration, producing a delayed ramp. Add a ‘breakout kicker’ that boosts days 3–15 forecasts when price makes fresh highs.",
    "Excess mean-reversion in forecasts: the model repeatedly pulls prices back toward ~68–71 even when realized prices persist in the mid/high-70s to low-80s. Reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility + trend are elevated.",
    "Underestimation of persistence: once prices move to a higher level, the model predicts partial retracement that does not occur (true path stays elevated). Increase level persistence (higher AR term) conditional on consecutive up days / higher-highs.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: near-term (1–3d) errors are modest; medium/long horizon (7–30d) errors are strongly negative in these samples. Use a piecewise correction: minimal adjustment days 1–3, moderate uplift days 4–10, larger uplift days 11–30 in uptrends.",
    "Volatility/convexity miss: true outcomes show larger upside excursions than forecast distribution (right-tail underpricing). Add an asymmetric adjustment: amplify positive trend scenarios more than negative ones (e.g., trend-based convex uplift)."
  ]
}
